Changing a reactive source notifies its subscribers.

### Assignment

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    count $Int = 0
    count = 1
]

Assigning to `count` updates the source. Anything subscribed to `count` will know it may need to update.

### Mutable collection source

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    names ${String} = {"Priya"}

    ~names.push("Rob")
]

You need `~` to mutate the collection. Reactivity does not relax the access rules. Growable `push` is infallible in source; allocation exhaustion remains fatal. Typed fallible operations like `get`, `set` and `remove` still need `catch` or propagation through a compatible error slot. Checked numeric failure follows the enclosing handler or function contract. Private functions without an error slot propagate that implicit failure within the module without postfix `!` at each internal call. The generated `start` has built-in `Error!`, so compatible top-level `!` is legal; custom errors need explicit recovery. See @../progress (Progress) for current support.
